  "reason": "The records collectively provide several relevant but disconnected clues: cooling rate can kinetically retain an fcc structure (R4, R7), composition and mixing thermodynamics can control multiphase formation and C14 Laves phases (R2, R18), and phase decomposition or coexistence may affect coercivity (R3). Records involving Co–Fe–Ni–Ti-containing alloys also support thermal or compositional phase control (R1, R11, R12, R20). However, none identifies the FeCoNiTi fcc+C14 combination, the matched C14-dominant comparison composition, Fe/Co/Ni–Ti antiferromagnetic sublattice coupling, phase-resolved transition temperatures, or orbital/spin-canting-assisted domain-wall pinning. Thus the literature points generally toward quench-controlled multiphase magnetic alloys, but reconstructing the specific intervention and mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
